{
  "schema_version": "howl.exploration_result/v1",
  "exploration_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d",
  "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p4-divergent2",
  "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
  "originating_component": "operator",
  "authority": {
    "type": "ADVISORY",
    "executable": false
  },
  "candidates": [
    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d/candidates/0/0",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p4-divergent2",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Third-base coach send/hold decisions; owner is the third-base coach with an advance and analytics staff; the decision comes up in nearly every game whenever a runner could try for home on a single or fly ball; league-wide historical cases number in the thousands per season through public play-by-play and tracking feeds; the observable outcome is safe, out, or held and the scoring that followed; the simple baseline is the coach's judgment plus a fixed rule like \"send with two outs\"; the intervention is a pregame card estimating break-even send probability by out, score, inning, runner sprint speed, fielder arm strength and fielder distance.\nASSUMPTION: Public tracking data records fielder throw distance, arm strength and runner position at the moment of the send decision accurately enough to rebuild the counterfactual for held runners.\nASSUMPTION: Held runners are a selected sample, so retrospective tests will be biased unless they model what would have happened on holds.\nASSUMPTION: Criterion (c) may fail if sends with a real edge are rare per club-season and most decisions are already obvious.\nUNKNOWN: club_send_hold_baseline_policy\nUNKNOWN: public_tracking_coverage_for_held_runners\n\nIDEA: Starting pitcher removal timing; owner is the manager and pitching coach; the decision comes up every game, with several candidate moments per start; league-wide cases number in the thousands of starts per season, each with many decision points, in public pitch-by-pitch data; the observable outcome is run expectancy change after the pull or stay, plus later reliever workload; the simple baseline is a pitch-count or times-through-order rule; the intervention is a live estimate of the starter's current effectiveness from velocity, release and command drift compared with the specific available reliever.\nASSUMPTION: Within-game velocity and command decline predicts near-term run prevention beyond what times-through-order already captures.\nASSUMPTION: Counterfactual outcomes for the pitcher not used cannot be observed, so the evaluation relies on modeled rather than observed outcomes; this weakens (b).\nASSUMPTION: Workload carryover to later games, and the resulting injury risk, may outweigh single-game gains and is poorly observable in public data.\nUNKNOWN: club_current_pull_rule\nUNKNOWN: reliever_availability_ground_truth_for_own_club\n\nIDEA: Stolen base and pickoff attempt choices under the pitch-clock disengagement limits; owners are the first-base coach, the manager and the runner; the decision comes up many times per game; league-wide cases number in the thousands of attempts and far more non-attempts per season in public data; the observable outcome is success, caught, pickoff or balk, plus the base-out state; the simple baseline is a green light for runners above a sprint-speed threshold; the intervention is a per-matchup estimate combining pitcher time to plate, catcher pop time, disengagements remaining, count and lead distance.\nASSUMPTION: Lead distance and jump data are publicly available at enough resolution to model non-attempts, not just attempts.\nASSUMPTION: League-wide adaptation since the rule change may have already removed most of the edge, which threatens (c).\nUNKNOWN: public_lead_distance_data_availability\nUNKNOWN: club_baserunning_green_light_policy\n\nIDEA: Daily lineup rest-day selection for position players; owner is the manager with the performance staff; the decision comes up daily across a 162-game season; league-wide cases number in the thousands of player-days rested or played per season through public lineup cards; the observable outcome is next-game and next-week batted-ball quality, sprint speed trends, and later injured-list placements; the simple baseline is a fixed rest cadence or day-game-after-night-game rule; the intervention is a fatigue signal from public sprint speed, bat speed and exit velocity trends that suggests which days to rest.\nASSUMPTION: Public bat-speed and sprint-speed drift reflects fatigue rather than noise, swing changes or undisclosed minor injuries.\nASSUMPTION: Without private workload and medical data, (d) may fail because the club already holds much better information than the public signal.\nASSUMPTION: The value per correctly timed rest day may be too small to reach (c) unless it prevents injured-list stints.\nUNKNOWN: link_between_public_fatigue_signal_and_injury\nUNKNOWN: club_internal_rest_protocol\n\nIDEA: Promotions between minor-league levels; owner is the farm director; the decision comes up continuously across the season; league-wide cases number in the hundreds to thousands of promotions per season through public MiLB transactions; the observable outcome is performance at the new level and time to the next promotion or demotion; the simple baseline is promoting on surface stats or age-for-level; the intervention ranks promotion readiness using public Statcast-style minor-league tracking (where it exists), plate discipline and contact quality rather than surface outcomes.\nASSUMPTION: Promotion outcomes are confounded because only players already judged ready get promoted, so a retrospective test needs a quasi-experimental design.\nASSUMPTION: Public minor-league tracking coverage differs by level and year, which may break (d) at the lower levels.\nASSUMPTION: The value of better promotion timing may only show up years later, which delays observing (b).\nUNKNOWN: public_milb_tracking_coverage_by_level_year\nUNKNOWN: club_promotion_criteria\n\nIDEA: Waiver claims on designated-for-assignment players; owner is the front office; the decision comes up weekly or more often during the season; league-wide cases number in the hundreds of DFAs per season in public transaction logs; the observable outcome is whether claimed players produce value or are quickly DFA'd again; the simple baseline is claiming based on prior MLB results and options remaining; the intervention flags DFA'd players whose underlying pitch quality or contact metrics far exceed their surface results.\nASSUMPTION: Waiver priority and counterparty claims limit which players a club can actually acquire, which weakens control over the outcome.\nASSUMPTION: This overlaps the minor-league free agent triage family, which tied a prior-MLB-time rule, so the edge may again be small.\nUNKNOWN: claim_success_rate_given_waiver_priority\nUNKNOWN: differentiation_from_R001_result\n\nIDEA: Bullpen warm-up decisions, including relievers who warm but do not enter; owner is the manager and bullpen coach; the decision comes up most games; league-wide historical cases are poorly recorded in public data; the outcome is reliever effectiveness and availability on following days; the simple baseline is warming on fixed triggers like a baserunner in a late close game; the intervention estimates the cost of a warm-up against the likelihood the reliever will be needed.\nASSUMPTION: Criterion (b) likely fails because public data rarely records warm-ups that do not lead to an appearance.\nASSUMPTION: This could only be tested on the club's own private logs, which limits league-wide statistical power under (a).\nUNKNOWN: public_warmup_records_existence\n\nIDEA: Catcher assignment for each starting pitcher; owner is the manager; the decision comes up daily; league-wide cases number in the thousands of pitcher-catcher pairings per season through public lineups and pitch data; the observable outcome is called-strike rate, pitch results and runs allowed for each pairing; the simple baseline is a fixed personal-catcher arrangement or the regular starter catching; the intervention estimates pairing-specific effects on called strikes and pitch-mix execution.\nASSUMPTION: Pairing effects are distinct and stable beyond each catcher's individual framing skill; this may not hold.\nASSUMPTION: The automated ball-strike challenge system could shrink framing value, which threatens (c) going forward.\nUNKNOWN: pairing_effect_stability\nUNKNOWN: abs_impact_on_framing_value\n\nIDEA: Intentional walk, infield-in and bunt choices in close late-game situations; owner is the manager; the decision comes up a few times per week; league-wide cases number in the thousands per season in public play-by-play; the observable outcome is the change in win expectancy; the simple baseline is a generic win-expectancy chart; the intervention adjusts for the specific batter, on-deck hitter, pitcher and fielder ranges.\nASSUMPTION: Criterion (c) may fail because these decisions are already well studied and the gains left at the margin are small.\nASSUMPTION: Treating many small decisions as summing to a meaningful season value assumes they are independent and the effects do not cancel; this is unverified.\nUNKNOWN: residual_edge_over_public_win_expectancy_tables\n\nASSUMPTION: Across all families, a high number of league-wide cases does not guarantee power if the true edge per decision is tiny compared with game-outcome noise.\nASSUMPTION: The club may already run equivalent tools, so the real baseline may be stronger than any simple rule assumed here.\nUNKNOWN: cubs_existing_tooling_for_in_game_decisions\nUNKNOWN: supplied_evidence",
