      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p4-divergent2",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Third-base coach send/hold decisions. Owner: third-base coach, with input from the advance and analytics staff. Decision: send or hold a runner on singles and doubles with a play at the plate possible, several times per series. Cases: every club generates these each season, and public tracking data covers multiple seasons, so league-wide cases are numerous. Outcome: safe, out, or held, and the resulting run expectancy, all publicly observable. Baseline: the coach's judgment, or a rule keyed only to runner sprint speed. Intervention: a pre-pitch card combining runner sprint speed, each outfielder's public arm strength and transfer time, outs, inning, score and on-deck hitter quality, giving a break-even send probability per situation.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) partly, because only sends produce an observed safe/out outcome; holds are censored, so tests of the counterfactual send depend on modeled throw outcomes.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) possibly, because aggregate run value per club-season may be small unless current coaching is systematically too conservative or too aggressive.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (d) possibly, because clubs may already give coaches arm-strength cards; public data may not show what the coach actually used.\nUNKNOWN: league_wide_send_hold_case_count_per_season\nUNKNOWN: whether_public_tracking_records_held_runner_opportunities_consistently\n\nIDEA: Starting pitcher removal timing within a game. Owner: manager and pitching coach. Decision: remove or continue the starter at each batter boundary from the middle innings onward, every start. Cases: every start league-wide over many seasons, so the number of decision points is far larger than the number of starts. Outcome: the plate-appearance outcomes that follow, publicly observable. Baseline: a pitch-count threshold or times-through-order rule. Intervention: a per-batter estimate of starter-versus-reliever expected run value using within-game velocity and spin decay, upcoming hitter handedness and quality, and the reliever availability already known to the club.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) in the causal sense, because pitchers left in are selected for looking good, so naive retrospective comparisons are confounded.\nASSUMPTION: Overlaps with the explored bullpen-availability family; it is distinct only if the focus is on the starter's in-game decay signal rather than the bullpen's state.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) partly, because the long-run cost of starter workload, such as injury, is not observed within the game, so short-run run value may overstate the gain.\nUNKNOWN: magnitude_of_within_game_velocity_decay_signal_beyond_pitch_count\n\nIDEA: Infield-in versus infield-back with a runner on third and fewer than two outs. Owner: manager and infield coach. Decision: infield depth on each qualifying plate appearance, many times per season. Cases: the base-out state recurs throughout every season for all clubs, and public tracking includes fielder positioning, so cases are numerous. Outcome: whether the run scored, hit or out, and the batted-ball result, all public. Baseline: infield in late and close, back otherwise. Intervention: a batter- and pitcher-specific ground-ball and exit-velocity model plus runner speed, giving the break-even inning and score for each matchup.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) possibly, because the situation is frequent but the per-decision value difference may be small.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) partly, because public positioning data may not distinguish deliberate infield-in from shading, and the 2023 shift restrictions changed the feasible alignments, which shortens the comparable history.\nUNKNOWN: whether_public_positioning_data_labels_infield_in_reliably_across_seasons\n\nIDEA: Minor-league level-promotion timing. Owner: player development director and farm director. Decision: promote, hold or demote a player between minor-league levels, evaluated continually and acted on many times per season. Cases: all 30 organizations move many players between levels each season, and public minor-league transaction and stat records exist, so cases are numerous. Outcome: performance and strikeout/walk changes at the new level, and eventual MLB arrival, publicly observable with lags. Baseline: promote after a fixed time at a level or when surface stats such as OPS or ERA cross a threshold. Intervention: a model favoring stable, fast-stabilizing indicators (contact rate, chase rate, minor-league tracking where public, strikeout-minus-walk rate) and age-relative-to-level, estimating the probability of success at the next level.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) causally, because promotion decisions are selected on private information, so retrospective tests compare promoted players to held ones who differ in unobserved ways.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (b) partly, because the long-run development outcome is delayed by years and confounded by injuries and later coaching.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (d) possibly, because minor-league tracking data are less public than MLB data and access may be restricted.\nUNKNOWN: public_availability_of_minor_league_tracking_metrics_by_level_and_season\nUNKNOWN: whether_too_early_promotion_has_measurable_developmental_harm\n\nIDEA: Minor-league pitcher workload and progression limits. Owner: player development and the medical/performance staff. Decision: innings, pitch-count and rest limits for each developing pitcher, set every outing. Cases: a large number of outings each season across all organizations. Outcome: public injured-list placements, velocity changes and missed time, observable with noise. Baseline: a uniform innings-increase cap year over year or fixed pitch counts by level. Intervention: an individualized limit using public velocity trends within and across outings, age and workload history, flagging pitchers whose decline pattern resembles past injured comparables.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (b) badly, because minor-league injury reporting is incomplete and clubs may disguise injuries as roster moves.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) for causal tests, because workload choices respond to private medical information.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) uncertainly, because the payoff depends on rare high-cost injuries, which gives low statistical power even with many outings.\nUNKNOWN: completeness_of_public_minor_league_injured_list_data\n\nIDEA: Late-game defensive substitution. Owner: manager and bench coach. Decision: replace a weaker defender with a stronger one when leading late, many games per season. Cases: every club makes this choice, or passes on it, routinely, so league-wide cases are numerous. Outcome: subsequent defensive plays, the lost bat if the game is tied or extended, and the result, all publicly observable. Baseline: substitute when leading by a small margin in the final innings. Intervention: a per-situation estimate trading expected defensive runs saved over the remaining outs against the expected offensive loss if the substitute's spot comes up again, including extra-innings probability.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) possibly, because the remaining outs are few, so value per decision is small and only adds up if the baseline is badly miscalibrated.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) partly, because the substitute's defensive impact over a few outs is mostly unobservable noise; tests must rely on modeled defensive value.\nUNKNOWN: whether_current_league_practice_differs_measurably_from_model_break_even\n\nIDEA: Manager replay challenges on non-ball-strike plays. Owner: replay coordinator and manager. Decision: challenge or not on close plays at the bases, tag plays and catches, multiple opportunities per game. Cases: challenges occur league-wide many times per season and outcomes are published, so cases are numerous. Outcome: overturned, confirmed or stands, publicly reported. Baseline: challenge whenever the video room's confidence crosses a gut threshold, with conservative behavior early in games. Intervention: a break-even model combining overturn probability, the play's run or win-expectancy swing, the inning and the option value of retaining the challenge.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (d) partly, because the key input, the video room's own read, is private, and public data show only challenged plays, not unchallenged close plays.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) possibly, because overturn value is concentrated in a small number of high-leverage plays per club-season.\nASSUMPTION: Overlaps conceptually with the explored ABS challenge-strategy family; it is distinct only because the inputs are video confidence and play leverage rather than pitch location.\nUNKNOWN: availability_of_unchallenged_close_play_records\n\nIDEA: Intentional walk decisions after the automatic intentional walk rule. Owner: manager. Decision: issue an intentional walk in base-out and score states where it is possible, several times per month. Cases: league-wide intentional walks and comparable non-walk situations recur each season, so cases are numerous. Outcome: subsequent plate appearances and runs, publicly observable. Baseline: walk the dangerous hitter with first base open late in close games. Intervention: a matchup-specific run- and win-expectancy comparison using the batter and on-deck hitter's pitch-type vulnerabilities against the current pitcher, and the platoon and bench state.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (c) likely, because intentional walks are less frequent in modern play, so total value per club may be small.\nASSUMPTION: Fails (a) partly, because walks are chosen in non-random situations, which confounds naive retrospective comparisons.\nUNKNOWN: league_wide_intentional_walk_frequency_trend_since_rule_change\n\nIDEA: Cross-cutting test design that applies to all ideas above. Use decision-point-level retrospective evaluation, with propensity or situational matching where choices are confounded, and report break-even margins rather than claimed wins.\nASSUMPTION: Public tracking and play-by-play data are lawfully accessible and sufficiently complete for each decision type; this requires verification per source.\nASSUMPTION: A club-level advantage requires that Cubs practice actually differs from the model break-even; no public evidence was supplied either way.\nUNKNOWN: current_chicago_cubs_practice_for_each_decision_type\nUNKNOWN: supplied_evidence_none",
