      "assumptions": [
        "Fails (a) partly, because only sends produce an observed safe/out outcome; holds are censored, so tests of the counterfactual send depend on modeled throw outcomes.",
        "Fails (c) possibly, because aggregate run value per club-season may be small unless current coaching is systematically too conservative or too aggressive.",
        "Fails (d) possibly, because clubs may already give coaches arm-strength cards; public data may not show what the coach actually used.",
        "Fails (a) in the causal sense, because pitchers left in are selected for looking good, so naive retrospective comparisons are confounded.",
        "Overlaps with the explored bullpen-availability family; it is distinct only if the focus is on the starter's in-game decay signal rather than the bullpen's state.",
        "Fails (c) partly, because the long-run cost of starter workload, such as injury, is not observed within the game, so short-run run value may overstate the gain.",
        "Fails (c) possibly, because the situation is frequent but the per-decision value difference may be small.",
        "Fails (a) partly, because public positioning data may not distinguish deliberate infield-in from shading, and the 2023 shift restrictions changed the feasible alignments, which shortens the comparable history.",
        "Fails (a) causally, because promotion decisions are selected on private information, so retrospective tests compare promoted players to held ones who differ in unobserved ways.",
        "Fails (b) partly, because the long-run development outcome is delayed by years and confounded by injuries and later coaching.",
        "Fails (d) possibly, because minor-league tracking data are less public than MLB data and access may be restricted.",
        "Fails (b) badly, because minor-league injury reporting is incomplete and clubs may disguise injuries as roster moves.",
        "Fails (a) for causal tests, because workload choices respond to private medical information.",
        "Fails (c) uncertainly, because the payoff depends on rare high-cost injuries, which gives low statistical power even with many outings.",
        "Fails (c) possibly, because the remaining outs are few, so value per decision is small and only adds up if the baseline is badly miscalibrated.",
        "Fails (a) partly, because the substitute's defensive impact over a few outs is mostly unobservable noise; tests must rely on modeled defensive value.",
        "Fails (d) partly, because the key input, the video room's own read, is private, and public data show only challenged plays, not unchallenged close plays.",
        "Fails (c) possibly, because overturn value is concentrated in a small number of high-leverage plays per club-season.",
        "Overlaps conceptually with the explored ABS challenge-strategy family; it is distinct only because the inputs are video confidence and play leverage rather than pitch location.",
        "Fails (c) likely, because intentional walks are less frequent in modern play, so total value per club may be small.",
        "Fails (a) partly, because walks are chosen in non-random situations, which confounds naive retrospective comparisons.",
        "Public tracking and play-by-play data are lawfully accessible and sufficiently complete for each decision type; this requires verification per source.",
        "A club-level advantage requires that Cubs practice actually differs from the model break-even; no public evidence was supplied either way."
