          "kind": "IDEA",
          "text": "Problem: scheduling rest days for everyday position players during the season. This is distinct from the explored travel planning family because the trigger is player performance and fatigue signals rather than travel. Owner: the manager and performance staff. Decision: which regular sits on which day, made many times per team-season. Cases: every regular's games-played sequence league-wide is public. Observable outcome: performance and sprint-speed trends after rest compared with continued play, plus injured-list placements. Baseline: rest each regular on a fixed cadence or on day games after night games. Intervention: rest triggered by public signals such as sprint-speed decline, bat-speed decline, and the opposing starter's handedness.",
