        {
          "id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d/candidates/0/2/claim/38",
          "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d/candidates/0/2",
          "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
          "text": "Across all ideas above, the minor-league ideas depend on public data completeness, which has not been verified. No statistics or Cubs-specific claims are made, and no supplied evidence was provided to support any line.",
          "status": "UNVERIFIED",
          "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_refs": [
        "objective_context"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "Public tracking data records fielder starting positions precisely enough and for enough of each season to separate alignment effects from batted-ball quality.",
        "Rule constraints leave enough room for alignment choices that the gap between a tuned and a default alignment is meaningful over a season. This could fail (c) if most clubs already use near-optimal legal alignments.",
        "Batters do not change their spray behavior quickly in response to alignment. If they do, the retrospective test overstates the gain.",
        "Promotion timing has a causal effect on development rather than just reflecting talent. Promoted players are selected, so a naive comparison will be confounded. This fails (a)'s usefulness unless a credible comparison design exists.",
        "Public minor-league tracking data is consistent enough across levels and seasons to serve as pre-decision information. This is a risk to (d).",
        "The value at stake accumulates across many prospects to something meaningful for one club. This is plausible for (c), but outcomes lag by years.",
        "Public injured-list records for minor-league pitchers capture injuries reliably. Underreporting would break (b).",
        "Workload is a meaningful driver of injury compared with unobserved biomechanics. If not, the intervention has a low ceiling, similar to the R001 result.",
        "Velocity data is publicly available at enough minor-league levels to be a pre-decision input.",
        "The retrospective test cannot observe what plan a hitter was given. Only the mismatch between hitter tendencies and pitcher tendencies can be measured, so the test checks for opportunity rather than the effect of the intervention. This is a failure on (b) for the intervention itself.",
        "Hitters can execute plan changes within a game without losing performance elsewhere.",
        "Opposing pitchers do not adapt within the series. This overlaps with the explored pitch-sequence family and needs separation.",
        "Enough conversions happen league-wide each season for statistical power. This is likely marginal on (a), and conversions to pitcher may be too rare.",
        "Public minor-league defensive metrics exist and are reliable. If not, (b) fails.",
        "This is included as a challenge case. It meets (a) and (d) partly, but fails timeliness on (b) because outcomes lag by five or more years. Public amateur data may also be thin compared with private scouting, which weakens (d).",
        "Public consensus rankings already incorporate most public information, which would leave little room above the baseline.",
        "Rest decisions are largely driven by matchups and minor injuries that are not public. This confounds a retrospective test and hurts (b).",
        "The performance difference from optimized rest timing adds up to meaningful value over a season. This is uncertain on (c) and may be small.",
        "Across all ideas above, the minor-league ideas depend on public data completeness, which has not been verified. No statistics or Cubs-specific claims are made, and no supplied evidence was provided to support any line."
      ],
      "unresolved_issues": [
        "Public tracking data records fielder starting positions precisely enough and for enough of each season to separate alignment effects from batted-ball quality.",
        "Rule constraints leave enough room for alignment choices that the gap between a tuned and a default alignment is meaningful over a season. This could fail (c) if most clubs already use near-optimal legal alignments.",
        "Batters do not change their spray behavior quickly in response to alignment. If they do, the retrospective test overstates the gain.",
        "Promotion timing has a causal effect on development rather than just reflecting talent. Promoted players are selected, so a naive comparison will be confounded. This fails (a)'s usefulness unless a credible comparison design exists.",
        "Public minor-league tracking data is consistent enough across levels and seasons to serve as pre-decision information. This is a risk to (d).",
        "The value at stake accumulates across many prospects to something meaningful for one club. This is plausible for (c), but outcomes lag by years.",
        "Public injured-list records for minor-league pitchers capture injuries reliably. Underreporting would break (b).",
        "Workload is a meaningful driver of injury compared with unobserved biomechanics. If not, the intervention has a low ceiling, similar to the R001 result.",
        "Velocity data is publicly available at enough minor-league levels to be a pre-decision input.",
        "The retrospective test cannot observe what plan a hitter was given. Only the mismatch between hitter tendencies and pitcher tendencies can be measured, so the test checks for opportunity rather than the effect of the intervention. This is a failure on (b) for the intervention itself.",
        "Hitters can execute plan changes within a game without losing performance elsewhere.",
        "Opposing pitchers do not adapt within the series. This overlaps with the explored pitch-sequence family and needs separation.",
        "Enough conversions happen league-wide each season for statistical power. This is likely marginal on (a), and conversions to pitcher may be too rare.",
        "Public minor-league defensive metrics exist and are reliable. If not, (b) fails.",
        "This is included as a challenge case. It meets (a) and (d) partly, but fails timeliness on (b) because outcomes lag by five or more years. Public amateur data may also be thin compared with private scouting, which weakens (d).",
        "Public consensus rankings already incorporate most public information, which would leave little room above the baseline.",
        "Rest decisions are largely driven by matchups and minor injuries that are not public. This confounds a retrospective test and hurts (b).",
        "The performance difference from optimized rest timing adds up to meaningful value over a season. This is uncertain on (c) and may be small.",
        "Across all ideas above, the minor-league ideas depend on public data completeness, which has not been verified. No statistics or Cubs-specific claims are made, and no supplied evidence was provided to support any line.",
        "Problem: infield depth and lateral alignment within the current shift restrictions. Owner: the defensive coordinator and advance staff. Decision: the pre-pitch infield placement for each plate appearance, made thousands of times per team-season. Cases: every ball in play league-wide with tracked fielder starting positions, for each season since the restrictions began. Observable outcome: batted-ball result (out, hit, extra bases) given launch characteristics and starting positions. Baseline: the default alignment by batter handedness and pull tendency. Intervention: a per-batter, per-pitcher-type alignment recommender, checked retrospectively against the default on held-out seasons.",
        "public_fielder_start_position_coverage_by_season",
        "cubs_current_infield_alignment_policy",
        "Problem: timing of level-to-level minor-league promotions (for example High-A to Double-A), excluding minor-league-to-MLB call-ups, which were already explored. Owner: the farm director. Decision: whether to promote a prospect now or hold them, made many times per organization each season. Cases: every intra-minor-league promotion league-wide, reconstructable from public transaction logs and level-by-level stat lines. Observable outcome: performance at the next level over a fixed window, plus later public outcomes such as reaching MLB or stalling. Baseline: promote after a fixed playing-time threshold combined with an age-for-level rule. Intervention: a model using public underlying indicators (strikeout and walk rates, contact quality where available) to flag early-promote and hold candidates.",
        "public_minor_league_transaction_completeness",
        "public_minor_league_statcast_coverage_by_level_and_season",
        "Problem: year-over-year workload progression for minor-league pitchers. Owner: the pitching coordinator and medical staff. Decision: each pitcher's innings or pitch-count ceiling and in-season pacing, set for every pitcher in an organization each year. Cases: thousands of pitcher-seasons league-wide with public innings, appearance and pitch-count logs. Observable outcome: publicly reported injured-list placements and next-season availability. Baseline: a fixed percentage increase over the prior season's innings. Intervention: a pacing rule that also uses public signals such as velocity trends, pitches per inning, and short-rest appearances.",
        "minor_league_il_reporting_completeness",
        "public_minor_league_pitch_count_availability",
        "Problem: individualized hitter approach plans (swing or take by count and pitch zone) against a specific opposing starter. Owner: the hitting coaches. Decision: the pre-game plan for each hitter in each game, made daily. Cases: every plate appearance league-wide, with public pitch-level data on swing decisions. Observable outcome: swing-decision quality and run value per plate appearance against that pitcher type. Baseline: a uniform team approach adjusted only for pitcher handedness. Intervention: plans that match each hitter's demonstrated swing-decision weaknesses to the opposing pitcher's location and count tendencies.",
        "cubs_existing_hitter_plan_process",
        "Problem: converting minor-league players to new positions (for example shortstop to second base or center field, or position player to pitcher). Owner: the player development director. Decision: whether and when to convert, made a moderate number of times per organization each year. Cases: conversions league-wide are identifiable from public appearance-by-position logs, but they number far fewer than promotions. Observable outcome: defensive metrics at the new position where public, plus offensive trajectory and progression to MLB. Baseline: convert when a player's age-for-level and error or range proxies fall below a threshold. Intervention: a model predicting the defensive value gain from a move using public batted-ball and sprint data.",
        "count_of_public_identifiable_position_conversions_per_season",
        "Problem: choosing which player to take within a draft round, as distinct from the explored bonus-pool allocation. Owner: the scouting director. Decision: hundreds of picks league-wide each year, including about one per round for each club. Cases: many draft classes of public pick lists linked to public pro outcomes. Observable outcome: progress through the minors and MLB value, observed only after many years. Baseline: consensus public prospect rankings. Intervention: a model reweighting public amateur performance data against the consensus.",
        "historical_public_consensus_ranking_archives_availability",
        "public_amateur_tracking_data_coverage",
        "Problem: scheduling rest days for everyday position players during the season. This is distinct from the explored travel planning family because the trigger is player performance and fatigue signals rather than travel. Owner: the manager and performance staff. Decision: which regular sits on which day, made many times per team-season. Cases: every regular's games-played sequence league-wide is public. Observable outcome: performance and sprint-speed trends after rest compared with continued play, plus injured-list placements. Baseline: rest each regular on a fixed cadence or on day games after night games. Intervention: rest triggered by public signals such as sprint-speed decline, bat-speed decline, and the opposing starter's handedness.",
        "public_bat_speed_and_sprint_speed_game_level_availability",
        "cubs_current_rest_policy"
      ],
