    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d/candidates/0/3",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p4-divergent2",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Problem: third-base-coach send/hold choices on runners trying to score from second on a single or from first on a double. Owner: third-base coach, with advance and analytics staff preparing per-opponent cards. Decision: send or hold, made many times per season. Historical cases: league-wide, every such play in tracked MLB games across multiple seasons. Observable outcome: runner safe, out, or held, plus the later run expectancy of the resulting base-out state. Simple baseline: the coach's current tendency, or a rule based on runner speed tier and outfielder depth. Intervention: a pregame card giving send thresholds by opposing outfielder arm, transfer time, depth, runner sprint speed, outs and score state.\nASSUMPTION: Public tracking data records outfielder fielding position, throw speed and runner speed precisely enough to rebuild the conditions at the moment of decision.\nASSUMPTION: Held runners' counterfactual outcomes can be estimated without bias, even though holds are only observed when the coach chose to hold, so selection bias is severe.\nASSUMPTION: A coach can use a simple per-opponent card in a split-second decision. If not, criterion (d) fails at execution even if the analysis succeeds.\nASSUMPTION: The total value over a season is meaningful rather than a few runs. This must be measured, not assumed, and criterion (c) may fail.\nUNKNOWN: public_dataset_coverage_of_send_hold_events\nUNKNOWN: cubs_current_send_hold_policy\nUNKNOWN: magnitude_of_league_wide_send_hold_inefficiency\n\nIDEA: Problem: outfielder throw-target choices, such as throwing home versus hitting the cutoff to hold the trailing runner. Owner: outfield coach and defensive coordinator. Decision: throw target on each runner-advance play, made many times per season. Historical cases: league-wide, all outfield hits with runners on in tracked games. Observable outcome: lead runner out or safe, trailing runner advanced or not, and run expectancy after the play. Simple baseline: always throw to the lead runner's base, or coach discretion. Intervention: a pregame matrix of throw targets by fielder arm, runner speeds and base-out state.\nASSUMPTION: Throw target is identifiable in public data. It may need video labeling, which raises cost and threatens criterion (b).\nASSUMPTION: Fielders can follow a prepared matrix under game conditions.\nASSUMPTION: This is distinct from positioning work, because it concerns the decision after the catch rather than alignment.\nUNKNOWN: public_labeling_of_throw_targets\nUNKNOWN: value_per_season_of_throw_target_optimization\n\nIDEA: Problem: runner tag-up and advance decisions on fly balls and balls in the dirt. Owner: baserunning coordinator and first- and third-base coaches. Decision: go or stay, made many times per season. Historical cases: league-wide, every fly ball with a runner on third or second and fewer than two outs, plus pitches in the dirt with runners on. Observable outcome: advance, out, or stay, and the resulting run expectancy. Simple baseline: a fixed rule such as \"tag on any medium-depth fly,\" or current club tendencies. Intervention: opponent-specific go thresholds using catcher block and transfer metrics, outfielder arm and catch-point depth, and runner speed.\nASSUMPTION: Catcher blocking and outfielder throwing metrics are stable enough within a season to justify thresholds specific to each opponent.\nASSUMPTION: Runners who stay are not mostly cases where going was obviously wrong, so the comparison is not trivially confounded.\nASSUMPTION: This does not substantially overlap already explored stolen-base or baserunner families. That needs a reviewer's check.\nUNKNOWN: overlap_with_explored_baserunning_families\nUNKNOWN: public_availability_of_catcher_blocking_inputs\n\nIDEA: Problem: manager replay-challenge decisions on reviewable non-pitch calls such as tag plays, force plays and fair/foul calls. Owner: manager and replay-room staff. Decision: challenge or not, made several times per week per club. Historical cases: league-wide, every published replay review and outcome across seasons. Observable outcome: call overturned or confirmed, and loss of challenge capacity. Simple baseline: challenge whenever the replay room signals \"likely,\" or challenge late-game close plays only. Intervention: a calibration audit of replay-room confidence against overturn outcomes, with challenge thresholds that depend on leverage and challenges remaining.\nASSUMPTION: Public replay logs include enough play context to estimate leverage. Unchallenged missed calls are mostly unobservable, so criterion (b) is only partly met.\nASSUMPTION: The value at stake is meaningful. It may be small relative to other families, so criterion (c) is at risk.\nASSUMPTION: The replay room's internal confidence signals could be logged going forward. This is a prospective test, not a retrospective one.\nUNKNOWN: public_replay_log_granularity\nUNKNOWN: unchallenged_missed_call_rate\n\nIDEA: Problem: how much weight to give spring-training performance in Opening Day roster and role choices. Owner: front office and major-league staff. Decision: roster spots and roles for marginal players, made once per spring, with several cases per club and many league-wide. Historical cases: league-wide, players with spring data whose roster outcome and following-season performance are public. Observable outcome: regular-season performance and roster survival. Simple baseline: ignore spring stats entirely and rank by projection, or rank by spring surface stats. Intervention: test whether spring tracking data such as velocity, swing speed and contact quality, where publicly captured, adds signal beyond projections.\nASSUMPTION: Public spring tracking data exists for enough venues and seasons. Coverage gaps may break criteria (a) and (d).\nASSUMPTION: Selection bias can be handled. Players who made rosters got playing time and others did not, so outcomes are only observed for those selected.\nASSUMPTION: Marginal roster spots carry enough value to satisfy criterion (c). This may fail if their replacement-level differences are tiny.\nUNKNOWN: spring_tracking_data_public_coverage\nUNKNOWN: incremental_signal_over_projection_systems\n\nIDEA: Problem: converting minor-league starters to relievers, or relievers to starters, in player development. Owner: pitching development staff. Decision: role conversion, made several times per season per organization and many times league-wide. Historical cases: league-wide, minor-league pitchers whose role changed, identifiable from public game logs. Observable outcome: later level reached, MLB appearances and performance after the switch. Simple baseline: convert when a pitcher is old for the level and has a fastball-heavy profile or poor stamina. Intervention: a model using public minor-league tracking data and pitch-mix breadth to rank which pitchers are likely to gain from conversion.\nASSUMPTION: Public minor-league pitch tracking covers enough levels and years to train on.\nASSUMPTION: Role changes driven by injury or organizational need can be separated from performance-driven conversions. If not, the causal estimate is confounded.\nASSUMPTION: Outcomes are observed within a horizon useful to the club. Development lag may delay feedback by several years and weaken criterion (b).\nASSUMPTION: This differs enough from the explored call-up translation and pitch design families.\nUNKNOWN: public_minor_league_tracking_coverage_by_level\nUNKNOWN: identifiability_of_conversion_reasons\nUNKNOWN: cubs_current_conversion_criteria\n\nASSUMPTION: Across all of these ideas, league-wide retrospective tests measure average opportunity, not whether the Cubs specifically underperform. Club-specific tendencies need separate verification from public play-by-play data.\nUNKNOWN: whether_any_family_clears_meaningful_seasonal_value_threshold",
