    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d/candidates/0/4",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p4-divergent2",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Lineup batting-order construction (owner: manager with analytics staff; decision: daily order of the nine starters, about 162 times per club per season). Historical cases: every lineup card league-wide, publicly recorded per game in play-by-play archives. Observable outcome: plate appearances by lineup slot and runs scored. Simple baseline: a fixed template such as best on-base hitters at the top and the power hitter in a middle slot. Intervention: a run-expectancy simulator that suggests order swaps using projected platoon matchups and probable opposing relievers, plus a record of when the manager overrode it.\nASSUMPTION: Public analyses commonly treat batting-order effects as small per game; season-level value may fail criterion (c) even if criterion (a) is strongly satisfied.\nASSUMPTION: The counterfactual of runs under a different order cannot be observed, so retrospective tests depend on simulation rather than on outcome comparison; this weakens criterion (b).\nASSUMPTION: Order can affect player comfort or preparation in ways that public data does not capture.\nUNKNOWN: club_baseline_lineup_rule\nUNKNOWN: public_estimate_of_order_effect_size_for_validation\n\nIDEA: Manager replay challenge selection (owner: manager and replay-review staff; decision: whether to challenge a close call, many opportunities per club-season). Historical cases: every challenge league-wide since expanded replay, with outcomes in public records; declined opportunities are only partly observable. Observable outcome: call overturned, confirmed, or stands, and the change in run or win expectancy. Simple baseline: challenge when the replay coordinator's confidence feels high, saving challenges for late innings. Intervention: a calibration ledger of coordinator confidence versus result, plus a leverage-weighted threshold that changes by inning and by challenges remaining.\nASSUMPTION: Unchallenged close plays are not systematically logged in public data, so the denominator of opportunities is missing; this partially fails criterion (a) for the \"should have challenged\" side.\nASSUMPTION: Coordinator confidence would have to be recorded prospectively by the club; public data cannot reconstruct it.\nASSUMPTION: Value per overturned call may be modest; the season total for criterion (c) needs verification.\nUNKNOWN: public_dataset_of_unchallenged_close_plays\nUNKNOWN: overturn_rate_by_play_type_public_source\n\nIDEA: Position-player pitching and mop-up allocation (owner: manager and pitching coach; decision: who pitches in low-leverage blowout innings, many times per club-season). Historical cases: every low-leverage relief inning league-wide, with leverage measurable from public play-by-play. Observable outcome: runs allowed in the blowout, plus the workload and next-days availability of affected relievers. Simple baseline: use the last reliever on the depth chart, or a position player past a fixed run-differential threshold. Intervention: a rule that weighs comeback probability against the expected cost of reliever fatigue over the following series.\nASSUMPTION: The downstream fatigue cost is inferred rather than observed; outcome attribution for criterion (b) is weak.\nASSUMPTION: Eligibility rules for position-player pitching have changed over time, which shrinks the comparable pool for criterion (a).\nASSUMPTION: Overlap with the explored \"opposing bullpen availability\" family is partial; this idea concerns the club's own bullpen.\nUNKNOWN: current_position_player_pitching_eligibility_rule\nUNKNOWN: measurable_link_between_mop_up_usage_and_next_day_performance\n\nIDEA: Affiliate level-to-level promotion timing within the minor leagues (owner: farm director; decision: when to promote a prospect from one minor-league level to the next, dozens of moves per organization-season). Historical cases: thousands of public level changes league-wide in MiLB transaction and stat records. Observable outcome: performance and age-relative-to-level at the new level, and eventual MLB arrival. Simple baseline: promote after a fixed time-at-level or a statistical threshold. Intervention: a public-data hazard model of how hitters and pitchers struggle or adjust after promotion, used to flag prospects who are likely ready early or too late.\nASSUMPTION: Promotion choices are confounded by private scouting, injury, and roster need, so a retrospective comparison is selection-biased; criterion (b) is observable but not causal.\nASSUMPTION: This differs from the explored \"call-up translation\" family because it concerns minor-to-minor moves; it could still be judged duplicative.\nASSUMPTION: Value for criterion (c) shows up over years rather than within one season.\nUNKNOWN: completeness_of_public_minor_league_transaction_history\nUNKNOWN: whether_public_minor_league_pitch_tracking_covers_all_levels_consistently\n\nIDEA: Amateur draft pick selection in middle and late rounds (owner: scouting director; decision: choice at each pick, many picks per club per year). Historical cases: every pick league-wide each year, with public draft records and later public career stats. Observable outcome: signing status, minor-league progression, and MLB appearances, observable after a long lag. Simple baseline: follow a consensus public prospect ranking, or use college performance thresholds. Intervention: a model that measures how far the club deviates from public consensus and which deviation types later paid off league-wide.\nASSUMPTION: Outcome lag of several years fails the timeliness side of criterion (b) for any near-term test.\nASSUMPTION: Signability and bonus negotiation are counterparty-driven and overlap the explored bonus-pool family.\nASSUMPTION: Archived public consensus rankings from before each draft may be incomplete for later rounds; this threatens criterion (d).\nUNKNOWN: archived_pre_draft_public_rankings_depth\nUNKNOWN: public_amateur_performance_data_coverage\n\nIDEA: Late-inning defensive replacement decisions (owner: manager and bench coach; decision: whether to substitute a better fielder when protecting a lead, many times per club-season). Historical cases: every late-game defensive substitution, plus every non-substitution, reconstructable from public play-by-play and lineup changes. Observable outcome: balls in play to the replaced position and runs allowed in the remaining innings. Simple baseline: always replace a designated weak fielder when leading in the late innings. Intervention: a leverage-aware rule that includes the cost of losing the original bat if the game goes to extra innings.\nASSUMPTION: Few balls reach any one position per inning, so the per-decision signal is noisy; criterion (a) may hold while effective statistical power stays low.\nASSUMPTION: Overlap with the explored \"bench\" families needs checking.\nUNKNOWN: public_fielding_run_value_granularity_by_inning\nUNKNOWN: extra_inning_frequency_needed_for_cost_term\n\nASSUMPTION: The unconventional element across these ideas is ranking decision families by statistical power and observability before building tools; that ranking itself still needs verification.\nUNKNOWN: club_simple_rule_for_each_decision\nUNKNOWN: minimum_detectable_effect_per_family",
      "condition": "dream",
