    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d/candidates/0/5",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-212841-eccdc1b3971d",
      "parent_request_id": "cubs-r003-p4-divergent2",
      "objective": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "text": "IDEA: Problem=manager replay challenges on non-ABS plays (safe/out, fair/foul, catch/trap, HBP); owner=manager with replay coordinator; decision=challenge or let the call stand, at a frequency of several plausible windows per week; historical cases=every challenged play league-wide each season since expanded replay began, plus unchallenged close plays recoverable from public tracking and video logs; outcome=overturned or confirmed, published per play; baseline=challenge when the replay coordinator's video looks favorable, adjusted for inning and remaining challenges; intervention=a model that combines play type, call type, camera-angle availability, leverage and remaining-challenge state to rank challenge value.\nASSUMPTION: (a) holds for challenged plays, but unchallenged close calls are a selected and incomplete sample, so counterfactual overturn rates for skipped challenges may be biased.\nASSUMPTION: (c) may fail because overturn value per play is small and most clubs may already capture most of it, leaving a low ceiling like the earlier minor-league free agent result.\nASSUMPTION: (d) may fail because the most informative input, the coordinator's live view of angles, is private and fast, so a public-data model may only tie the human baseline.\nUNKNOWN: league-wide count of unchallenged plays that would have been overturned\nUNKNOWN: Cubs challenge success rate relative to league\nIDEA: Problem=in-season promotions between minor-league levels (Low-A to High-A to Double-A to Triple-A); owner=farm director and player-development staff; decision=promote now, hold, or demote, made many times per season per organization; historical cases=every level change across all organizations each season, reconstructable from public transaction logs and level-by-level stat lines; outcome=post-move performance at the new level, plus eventual MLB arrival or release, both public; baseline=promote after a stretch of strong surface stats or by age-for-level convention; intervention=a promotion-readiness score from public pitch-level and batted-ball data at levels where it exists, such as Triple-A and parts of the Florida State League, compared against the age-and-OPS rule.\nASSUMPTION: This is distinct from MLB call-up translation because the decision is about minor-league development pacing, not about MLB roster value.\nASSUMPTION: (b) partly fails because the true outcome, long-run development, is observed years later and is confounded by injuries, mechanical changes and later promotions.\nASSUMPTION: (c) may fail because the value of earlier or later promotion per player may be small and hard to separate from player quality, which drives both the promotion and the outcome.\nASSUMPTION: Organizational reasons such as roster space at the next level, rehab assignments and protecting a player from Rule 5 drive many moves, so a public dataset sees decisions without their constraints.\nUNKNOWN: completeness of public pitch-tracking coverage by minor-league level and season\nUNKNOWN: whether demotions are labeled consistently in public transaction feeds\nIDEA: Problem=undrafted amateur signings after the shortened draft; owner=amateur scouting director; decision=which undrafted college and junior-college players to sign at the capped bonus, made once a year across many players; historical cases=all undrafted signings league-wide since the draft was shortened, each with public amateur stats and, for many college players, public tracking data from summer leagues; outcome=minor-league performance and retention, which is public; baseline=sign the highest-ranked remaining players on public prospect boards, or players with strong conference stats; intervention=a model that combines public college stats, strength-of-schedule adjustment and summer-league tracking.\nASSUMPTION: This may repeat the minor-league free agent finding of a low ceiling, so it is a lower-priority family unless early outcomes vary widely.\nASSUMPTION: (a) is weak because the post-shortening seasons are few, and the outcome lag is long.\nASSUMPTION: (c) and the counterparty problem both apply, because a player chooses among competing offers and the club's choice does not guarantee a signing.\nUNKNOWN: count of undrafted signings per season league-wide since the draft was shortened\nUNKNOWN: availability and licensing of college and summer-league tracking data\nIDEA: Problem=choosing which regular to rest on which day; owner=manager with performance staff; decision=the daily lineup omission, made most days of the season; historical cases=every starter-absence day league-wide, inferable from public lineups and injury lists; outcome=the team result that day and the rested player's later performance and injured-list placements, all public; baseline=rest players by a fixed cadence, or on day games after night games, or against tough opposing starters; intervention=pick rest days by the expected run cost of the replacement, given the opposing starter's handedness and quality and the bench matchup.\nASSUMPTION: This may overlap the already explored travel and rest planning family; it differs only if the objective is matchup run cost rather than fatigue.\nASSUMPTION: (b) partly fails because the benefit of rest for injury prevention is not observable at the play level and is confounded by unreported minor injuries.\nASSUMPTION: (c) may be small per game, though it adds up across a season; that is the hypothesis to test, not a given.\nUNKNOWN: whether public lineups distinguish planned rest from undisclosed injury\nIDEA: Problem=intentional walks and unintentional-intentional pitch-around choices; owner=manager and pitching coach; decision=walk or pitch to a batter with a base open, faced several times per week; historical cases=every base-open, late-leverage plate appearance league-wide, with the choice inferable for automatic walks and approximately inferable from pitch location for pitch-arounds; outcome=play result and win-probability change, public; baseline=walk dangerous hitters with first base open late in close games; intervention=a matchup-specific run-expectancy comparison using the next hitter's projection and the platoon state.\nASSUMPTION: (c) likely fails because public research suggests these decisions carry little value per case, and clubs may have converged on them already; that claim needs verification and is not supplied here.\nASSUMPTION: Labeling pitch-arounds from pitch location adds measurement error that could swamp a small effect.\nUNKNOWN: league-wide frequency of automatic intentional walks since the automatic walk rule was adopted\nIDEA: Problem=bullpen warm-ups that are not followed by an appearance; owner=manager and bullpen coach; decision=whether to get a reliever up, made in most close games; outcome=reliever fatigue and next-day effectiveness; baseline=warm up the next reliever when the pitcher on the mound reaches a pitch count or allows baserunners; intervention=a warm-up trigger model.\nASSUMPTION: This is included as a deliberate failure case, because (b) fails: warm-up events and pitch counts are not reliably public, so a retrospective test lacks labels even though (a) and (c) may hold.\nASSUMPTION: The idea is viable only with the club's private bullpen logs, which conflicts with the public-data constraint, so it should be parked rather than tested.\nUNKNOWN: any public source that logs bullpen warm-ups\nIDEA: Problem=batting-order construction; owner=manager; decision=lineup order, set daily; historical cases=every lineup league-wide; outcome=runs scored, public; baseline=a conventional order with the best on-base hitters near the top; intervention=a simulation-optimized order.\nASSUMPTION: This is included as a (c) failure case: public research suggests that optimal and reasonable orders differ by little over a season, and the effect would be undetectable against noise even with strong (a); this needs citation before it is relied on.\nUNKNOWN: supplied evidence for any family above, because the evidence array is empty\nUNKNOWN: Cubs-specific baselines, which are not public and must not be assumed",
      "condition": "dream",
