      "assumptions": [
        "(a) holds for challenged plays, but unchallenged close calls are a selected and incomplete sample, so counterfactual overturn rates for skipped challenges may be biased.",
        "(c) may fail because overturn value per play is small and most clubs may already capture most of it, leaving a low ceiling like the earlier minor-league free agent result.",
        "(d) may fail because the most informative input, the coordinator's live view of angles, is private and fast, so a public-data model may only tie the human baseline.",
        "This is distinct from MLB call-up translation because the decision is about minor-league development pacing, not about MLB roster value.",
        "(b) partly fails because the true outcome, long-run development, is observed years later and is confounded by injuries, mechanical changes and later promotions.",
        "(c) may fail because the value of earlier or later promotion per player may be small and hard to separate from player quality, which drives both the promotion and the outcome.",
        "Organizational reasons such as roster space at the next level, rehab assignments and protecting a player from Rule 5 drive many moves, so a public dataset sees decisions without their constraints.",
        "This may repeat the minor-league free agent finding of a low ceiling, so it is a lower-priority family unless early outcomes vary widely.",
        "(a) is weak because the post-shortening seasons are few, and the outcome lag is long.",
        "(c) and the counterparty problem both apply, because a player chooses among competing offers and the club's choice does not guarantee a signing.",
        "This may overlap the already explored travel and rest planning family; it differs only if the objective is matchup run cost rather than fatigue.",
