  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "objective_context",
      "text": "Where could software create a meaningful baseball advantage for the Chicago Cubs? Second pass. A critique of the first pass found that most candidate decisions (qualifying offers, posting bids, arbitration hearings, postseason rosters, coach moves) have too few historical cases to test any tool against a club's simple rule, and many outcomes are driven by counterparties. Find decisions that combine: (a) many historical cases per season league-wide, so a retrospective test has statistical power; (b) an outcome that is publicly observable after the decision; (c) value at stake that adds up to something meaningful for one club over a season; (d) public information available before the decision that a simple rule might underuse. Include player-development, in-season management and roster decisions, and say where (a) to (d) would fail. OUTPUT GUIDANCE (format only): Each IDEA names the problem, owner, decision and its frequency, an estimate-free description of the number of historical cases, the observable outcome, the simple baseline, and the intervention. Follow with ASSUMPTION and UNKNOWN lines. Do not invent statistics or private Cubs information.",
      "facts": {},
      "provenance": {
        "source_type": "UNCLASSIFIED",
        "produced_by": "unknown",
        "source_ref": "",
        "independent_of_implementation": false,
        "represents_observed_reality": false
      }
    }
  ],
  "unresolved_assumptions": [
    "Public tracking data records fielder throw distance, arm strength and runner position at the moment of the send decision accurately enough to rebuild the counterfactual for held runners.",
    "Held runners are a selected sample, so retrospective tests will be biased unless they model what would have happened on holds.",
    "Criterion (c) may fail if sends with a real edge are rare per club-season and most decisions are already obvious.",
    "Within-game velocity and command decline predicts near-term run prevention beyond what times-through-order already captures.",
    "Counterfactual outcomes for the pitcher not used cannot be observed, so the evaluation relies on modeled rather than observed outcomes; this weakens (b).",
    "Workload carryover to later games, and the resulting injury risk, may outweigh single-game gains and is poorly observable in public data.",
    "Lead distance and jump data are publicly available at enough resolution to model non-attempts, not just attempts.",
    "League-wide adaptation since the rule change may have already removed most of the edge, which threatens (c).",
    "Public bat-speed and sprint-speed drift reflects fatigue rather than noise, swing changes or undisclosed minor injuries.",
    "Without private workload and medical data, (d) may fail because the club already holds much better information than the public signal.",
    "The value per correctly timed rest day may be too small to reach (c) unless it prevents injured-list stints.",
    "Promotion outcomes are confounded because only players already judged ready get promoted, so a retrospective test needs a quasi-experimental design.",
    "Public minor-league tracking coverage differs by level and year, which may break (d) at the lower levels.",
    "The value of better promotion timing may only show up years later, which delays observing (b).",
    "Waiver priority and counterparty claims limit which players a club can actually acquire, which weakens control over the outcome.",
    "This overlaps the minor-league free agent triage family, which tied a prior-MLB-time rule, so the edge may again be small.",
    "Criterion (b) likely fails because public data rarely records warm-ups that do not lead to an appearance.",
    "This could only be tested on the club's own private logs, which limits league-wide statistical power under (a).",
    "Pairing effects are distinct and stable beyond each catcher's individual framing skill; this may not hold.",
    "The automated ball-strike challenge system could shrink framing value, which threatens (c) going forward.",
    "Criterion (c) may fail because these decisions are already well studied and the gains left at the margin are small.",
    "Treating many small decisions as summing to a meaningful season value assumes they are independent and the effects do not cancel; this is unverified.",
    "Across all families, a high number of league-wide cases does not guarantee power if the true edge per decision is tiny compared with game-outcome noise.",
    "The club may already run equivalent tools, so the real baseline may be stronger than any simple rule assumed here.",
    "Fails (a) partly, because only sends produce an observed safe/out outcome; holds are censored, so tests of the counterfactual send depend on modeled throw outcomes.",
    "Fails (c) possibly, because aggregate run value per club-season may be small unless current coaching is systematically too conservative or too aggressive.",
    "Fails (d) possibly, because clubs may already give coaches arm-strength cards; public data may not show what the coach actually used.",
    "Fails (a) in the causal sense, because pitchers left in are selected for looking good, so naive retrospective comparisons are confounded.",
    "Overlaps with the explored bullpen-availability family; it is distinct only if the focus is on the starter's in-game decay signal rather than the bullpen's state.",
    "Fails (c) partly, because the long-run cost of starter workload, such as injury, is not observed within the game, so short-run run value may overstate the gain.",
    "Fails (c) possibly, because the situation is frequent but the per-decision value difference may be small.",
    "Fails (a) partly, because public positioning data may not distinguish deliberate infield-in from shading, and the 2023 shift restrictions changed the feasible alignments, which shortens the comparable history.",
    "Fails (a) causally, because promotion decisions are selected on private information, so retrospective tests compare promoted players to held ones who differ in unobserved ways.",
    "Fails (b) partly, because the long-run development outcome is delayed by years and confounded by injuries and later coaching.",
    "Fails (d) possibly, because minor-league tracking data are less public than MLB data and access may be restricted.",
    "Fails (b) badly, because minor-league injury reporting is incomplete and clubs may disguise injuries as roster moves.",
    "Fails (a) for causal tests, because workload choices respond to private medical information.",
    "Fails (c) uncertainly, because the payoff depends on rare high-cost injuries, which gives low statistical power even with many outings.",
    "Fails (c) possibly, because the remaining outs are few, so value per decision is small and only adds up if the baseline is badly miscalibrated.",
    "Fails (a) partly, because the substitute's defensive impact over a few outs is mostly unobservable noise; tests must rely on modeled defensive value.",
    "Fails (d) partly, because the key input, the video room's own read, is private, and public data show only challenged plays, not unchallenged close plays.",
    "Fails (c) possibly, because overturn value is concentrated in a small number of high-leverage plays per club-season.",
    "Overlaps conceptually with the explored ABS challenge-strategy family; it is distinct only because the inputs are video confidence and play leverage rather than pitch location.",
    "Fails (c) likely, because intentional walks are less frequent in modern play, so total value per club may be small.",
    "Fails (a) partly, because walks are chosen in non-random situations, which confounds naive retrospective comparisons.",
    "Public tracking and play-by-play data are lawfully accessible and sufficiently complete for each decision type; this requires verification per source.",
    "A club-level advantage requires that Cubs practice actually differs from the model break-even; no public evidence was supplied either way.",
    "Public tracking data records fielder starting positions precisely enough and for enough of each season to separate alignment effects from batted-ball quality.",
    "Rule constraints leave enough room for alignment choices that the gap between a tuned and a default alignment is meaningful over a season. This could fail (c) if most clubs already use near-optimal legal alignments.",
    "Batters do not change their spray behavior quickly in response to alignment. If they do, the retrospective test overstates the gain.",
    "Promotion timing has a causal effect on development rather than just reflecting talent. Promoted players are selected, so a naive comparison will be confounded. This fails (a)'s usefulness unless a credible comparison design exists.",
    "Public minor-league tracking data is consistent enough across levels and seasons to serve as pre-decision information. This is a risk to (d).",
    "The value at stake accumulates across many prospects to something meaningful for one club. This is plausible for (c), but outcomes lag by years.",
    "Public injured-list records for minor-league pitchers capture injuries reliably. Underreporting would break (b).",
    "Workload is a meaningful driver of injury compared with unobserved biomechanics. If not, the intervention has a low ceiling, similar to the R001 result.",
    "Velocity data is publicly available at enough minor-league levels to be a pre-decision input.",
    "The retrospective test cannot observe what plan a hitter was given. Only the mismatch between hitter tendencies and pitcher tendencies can be measured, so the test checks for opportunity rather than the effect of the intervention. This is a failure on (b) for the intervention itself.",
    "Hitters can execute plan changes within a game without losing performance elsewhere.",
    "Opposing pitchers do not adapt within the series. This overlaps with the explored pitch-sequence family and needs separation.",
    "Enough conversions happen league-wide each season for statistical power. This is likely marginal on (a), and conversions to pitcher may be too rare.",
    "Public minor-league defensive metrics exist and are reliable. If not, (b) fails.",
    "This is included as a challenge case. It meets (a) and (d) partly, but fails timeliness on (b) because outcomes lag by five or more years. Public amateur data may also be thin compared with private scouting, which weakens (d).",
    "Public consensus rankings already incorporate most public information, which would leave little room above the baseline.",
    "Rest decisions are largely driven by matchups and minor injuries that are not public. This confounds a retrospective test and hurts (b).",
    "The performance difference from optimized rest timing adds up to meaningful value over a season. This is uncertain on (c) and may be small.",
    "Across all ideas above, the minor-league ideas depend on public data completeness, which has not been verified. No statistics or Cubs-specific claims are made, and no supplied evidence was provided to support any line.",
    "Public tracking data records outfielder fielding position, throw speed and runner speed precisely enough to rebuild the conditions at the moment of decision.",
    "Held runners' counterfactual outcomes can be estimated without bias, even though holds are only observed when the coach chose to hold, so selection bias is severe.",
    "A coach can use a simple per-opponent card in a split-second decision. If not, criterion (d) fails at execution even if the analysis succeeds.",
    "The total value over a season is meaningful rather than a few runs. This must be measured, not assumed, and criterion (c) may fail.",
    "Throw target is identifiable in public data. It may need video labeling, which raises cost and threatens criterion (b).",
    "Fielders can follow a prepared matrix under game conditions.",
    "This is distinct from positioning work, because it concerns the decision after the catch rather than alignment.",
    "Catcher blocking and outfielder throwing metrics are stable enough within a season to justify thresholds specific to each opponent.",
    "Runners who stay are not mostly cases where going was obviously wrong, so the comparison is not trivially confounded.",
    "This does not substantially overlap already explored stolen-base or baserunner families. That needs a reviewer's check.",
    "Public replay logs include enough play context to estimate leverage. Unchallenged missed calls are mostly unobservable, so criterion (b) is only partly met.",
    "The value at stake is meaningful. It may be small relative to other families, so criterion (c) is at risk.",
    "The replay room's internal confidence signals could be logged going forward. This is a prospective test, not a retrospective one.",
    "Public spring tracking data exists for enough venues and seasons. Coverage gaps may break criteria (a) and (d).",
    "Selection bias can be handled. Players who made rosters got playing time and others did not, so outcomes are only observed for those selected.",
    "Marginal roster spots carry enough value to satisfy criterion (c). This may fail if their replacement-level differences are tiny.",
    "Public minor-league pitch tracking covers enough levels and years to train on.",
    "Role changes driven by injury or organizational need can be separated from performance-driven conversions. If not, the causal estimate is confounded.",
    "Outcomes are observed within a horizon useful to the club. Development lag may delay feedback by several years and weaken criterion (b).",
    "This differs enough from the explored call-up translation and pitch design families.",
    "Across all of these ideas, league-wide retrospective tests measure average opportunity, not whether the Cubs specifically underperform. Club-specific tendencies need separate verification from public play-by-play data.",
    "Public analyses commonly treat batting-order effects as small per game; season-level value may fail criterion (c) even if criterion (a) is strongly satisfied.",
    "The counterfactual of runs under a different order cannot be observed, so retrospective tests depend on simulation rather than on outcome comparison; this weakens criterion (b).",
    "Order can affect player comfort or preparation in ways that public data does not capture.",
    "Unchallenged close plays are not systematically logged in public data, so the denominator of opportunities is missing; this partially fails criterion (a) for the \"should have challenged\" side.",
    "Coordinator confidence would have to be recorded prospectively by the club; public data cannot reconstruct it.",
    "Value per overturned call may be modest; the season total for criterion (c) needs verification.",
    "The downstream fatigue cost is inferred rather than observed; outcome attribution for criterion (b) is weak.",
    "Eligibility rules for position-player pitching have changed over time, which shrinks the comparable pool for criterion (a).",
    "Overlap with the explored \"opposing bullpen availability\" family is partial; this idea concerns the club's own bullpen.",
    "Promotion choices are confounded by private scouting, injury, and roster need, so a retrospective comparison is selection-biased; criterion (b) is observable but not causal.",
    "This differs from the explored \"call-up translation\" family because it concerns minor-to-minor moves; it could still be judged duplicative.",
    "Value for criterion (c) shows up over years rather than within one season.",
    "Outcome lag of several years fails the timeliness side of criterion (b) for any near-term test.",
    "Signability and bonus negotiation are counterparty-driven and overlap the explored bonus-pool family.",
    "Archived public consensus rankings from before each draft may be incomplete for later rounds; this threatens criterion (d).",
    "Few balls reach any one position per inning, so the per-decision signal is noisy; criterion (a) may hold while effective statistical power stays low.",
    "Overlap with the explored \"bench\" families needs checking.",
    "The unconventional element across these ideas is ranking decision families by statistical power and observability before building tools; that ranking itself still needs verification.",
    "(a) holds for challenged plays, but unchallenged close calls are a selected and incomplete sample, so counterfactual overturn rates for skipped challenges may be biased.",
    "(c) may fail because overturn value per play is small and most clubs may already capture most of it, leaving a low ceiling like the earlier minor-league free agent result.",
    "(d) may fail because the most informative input, the coordinator's live view of angles, is private and fast, so a public-data model may only tie the human baseline.",
    "This is distinct from MLB call-up translation because the decision is about minor-league development pacing, not about MLB roster value.",
    "(b) partly fails because the true outcome, long-run development, is observed years later and is confounded by injuries, mechanical changes and later promotions.",
    "(c) may fail because the value of earlier or later promotion per player may be small and hard to separate from player quality, which drives both the promotion and the outcome.",
    "Organizational reasons such as roster space at the next level, rehab assignments and protecting a player from Rule 5 drive many moves, so a public dataset sees decisions without their constraints.",
    "This may repeat the minor-league free agent finding of a low ceiling, so it is a lower-priority family unless early outcomes vary widely.",
    "(a) is weak because the post-shortening seasons are few, and the outcome lag is long.",
    "(c) and the counterparty problem both apply, because a player chooses among competing offers and the club's choice does not guarantee a signing.",
    "This may overlap the already explored travel and rest planning family; it differs only if the objective is matchup run cost rather than fatigue.",
    "(b) partly fails because the benefit of rest for injury prevention is not observable at the play level and is confounded by unreported minor injuries.",
    "(c) may be small per game, though it adds up across a season; that is the hypothesis to test, not a given.",
    "(c) likely fails because public research suggests these decisions carry little value per case, and clubs may have converged on them already; that claim needs verification and is not supplied here.",
    "Labeling pitch-arounds from pitch location adds measurement error that could swamp a small effect.",
    "This is included as a deliberate failure case, because (b) fails: warm-up events and pitch counts are not reliably public, so a retrospective test lacks labels even though (a) and (c) may hold.",
    "The idea is viable only with the club's private bullpen logs, which conflicts with the public-data constraint, so it should be parked rather than tested.",
    "This is included as a (c) failure case: public research suggests that optimal and reasonable orders differ by little over a season, and the effect would be undetectable against noise even with strong (a); this needs citation before it is relied on."
